-- machine-in-hyperactive (2026-11-21): Avik: "yes we definitely want it written in hyperactive. .the
-- only question is how to get there". The machine is the paper's §4.5 as definitions of the language:
-- the rules a function of the two cells that met and nothing else, the memory (what waits at a
-- wire) separate from the rules, and the executor (which ready pair goes next) separate from both, so
-- that an executor for any hardware is a different choice over the same rules. Known (2026-11-21):
-- the smallest such machine is written, module kernel under lib, names nk-*: values are a closure (a
-- body and the wires of its environment, as the old machine's λ cell), a number, a reference to a
-- held wire; operations are apply, increment, duplicate, erase, forward. nk-rule is every rule;
-- nk-do is the memory; nk-step is the one-core executor's choice. It runs in the language: inc ((λg.
-- (λf. f (f x)) (λy. g y)) (λz. inc z)) at 5 gives 8, f a closure with a part, held once and read
-- twice; a term whose size is a run-time input gives 107 for 6 applications at 100. Proven: the
-- memory's two laws (nk-get-set: a wire just written reads as what was written; nk-get-set-other:
-- writing one wire leaves every other wire's reading unchanged). Emitted through the C experiment,
-- the machine running a fixed term comes out as one C function, v0 + 3: the specializer runs the
-- machine away at build time, so the machine with the specializer compiles a net term to C. Not in
-- it: neutrals, dimensions, systems, composition, data, the freeing of held wires. Not proven: that
-- steps at different wires commute (wire names come from one counter, so two orders agree only up
-- to renaming of the fresh wires; names derived from the wire where the pair met would make it
-- exact); that a step preserves the meaning of the net, which needs the meaning of a net as a term
-- first. Learned about the tools: the checker refuses, with no message, a recursion that is not on
-- a constructor; the source finds every name across all modules, constructors included, so a new
-- name is checked with `hyperactive history NAME` before it is written; `view` loads the command
-- line's definitions as well, so a module is checked with `hyperactive check` on the file that
-- loads it.
machine-in-hyperactive : Node
machine-in-hyperactive = node "The machine is written in hyperactive: its rules, its memory and its executor separate definitions, each rule proven to preserve the meaning of the net and steps at different wires proven to commute." (cons language-implementation-remainder nil)
